 ## Background
-Pulsar was presented in 2021 by the authors of the paper "Pulsar: Efficient Sphere-based Neural Rendering" [S1]. It builds on recent insights in differentiable rendering and neural networks [S1]. The method was developed to address the computational bottlenecks of existing differentiable point-based renderers, specifically by limiting complexity in the projection step [S1]. It is implemented as open-source software with tight integration with PyTorch [S1].
+Pulsar was introduced in the paper "Pulsar: Efficient Sphere-based Neural Rendering" [S1]. It builds on recent insights in differentiable rendering and neural networks, aiming to limit complexity in the projection step to achieve high speed and scalability [S1]. The method was implemented as open-source software with tight integration with PyTorch, using highly optimized CUDA kernels [S1]. It has been used for applications ranging from 3D reconstruction to general neural rendering, including detailed head models with hair and eyes [S1].
 
